Jira 1 — Prepare Database for Schema Migration
Summary
Prepare Database for Schema Migration Execution
Description
Overview
Implement the pre-migration database preparation required before migrating existing production data from the current schema version to the new schema version.
This Jira covers the database preparation steps only. Actual business data migration from CSV will be covered in the subsequent Jira.
The implementation should be reusable for future schema migrations such as V2 → V3.
Scope
The migration preparation flow should:
Create/verify production backup or snapshot reference.
Preserve the current history state as an immutable schema-versioned archive.
Initialize the new active HISTORY table using the target schema.
Update MAIN table structure to the target schema.
Keep newly introduced mandatory/conditional columns permissive during the data migration phase.
Record migration execution/control information.
Implementation Details
The migration service should expose a reusable orchestration method:
public void migrateSchema(
        String entity,
        int fromSchemaVersion,
        int toSchemaVersion)
Preparation methods:
createMigrationBackup();

preservePreviousSchemaHistory(
        fromSchemaVersion);

initializeActiveHistory(
        toSchemaVersion);

updateMainSchema(
        toSchemaVersion);
Migration-specific rules must be read from the externalized migration definition YAML, rather than application.yml.
Example:
migrations/
├── v1-to-v2-schema-migration.yml
└── v2-to-v3-schema-migration.yml
The YAML contains configuration for all applicable entities, including:
from-version
to-version
applicable-columns
mandatory-columns
Expected State After Preparation
MAIN
→ Target schema, data not yet fully migrated

HISTORY
→ New target-schema active history

HISTORY_ARCHIVE_<previous-version>
→ Immutable copy of pre-migration state
Error Handling
Backup failure → stop migration.
History archive creation failure → stop migration.
Active HISTORY preparation failure → stop migration.
MAIN schema update failure → stop migration.
Migration must not proceed to CSV data migration if preparation is incomplete.
Migration status should reflect the failed stage.
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Backup/snapshot reference is recorded.
[ ] Previous HISTORY state is preserved as an immutable archive.
[ ] Target active HISTORY is initialized.
[ ] MAIN has the target schema required for data migration.
[ ] Final mandatory constraints are not applied at this stage.
[ ] Migration preparation failure prevents data migration.
[ ] Implementation is reusable for future schema versions.
Jira 2 — Implement MAIN Data Migration from CSV
Summary
Implement Bulk MAIN Data Migration from External CSV
Description
Overview
Implement the data migration component that reads business-provided migration data from CSV and updates existing MAIN records from the previous business/data version to the target version.
This Jira covers CSV processing, validation and MAIN data update.
The migration must not use BAU Create/Modify APIs because migration transformations may differ from normal business CRUD rules.
Flow
CSV
 ↓
Read Migration Data
 ↓
Validate CSV
 ↓
Validate Record Coverage
 ↓
Bulk Update MAIN
 ↓
Verify Update Results
Implementation Details
Suggested classes:
migration/
└── v1tov2/
    ├── MigrationDataReader
    ├── MigrationRepository
    └── MigrationException
Suggested methods:
readMigrationData();

validateMigrationData();

validateRecordCoverage();

updateMainData();

validateUpdateCounts();
The implementation should use the latest application/domain model where appropriate, but MAIN persistence should use explicit JDBC DML through JdbcTemplate.
Bulk Update
For the expected ~2,000 records, use JDBC batch processing:
jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate(
        updateSql,
        migrationRows,
        2000,
        this::setUpdateParameters);
The update should:
Update only applicable columns.
Preserve unchanged existing columns.
Populate new/replacement fields from the CSV.
Set the migrated business/data version to the target version.
Identify records using the existing business/entity ID.
Use parameterized SQL.
Example:
UPDATE customer
SET field_b = ?,
    field_c = ?,
    data_version = ?
WHERE customer_id = ?
  AND data_version = ?
Transaction
The actual migration execution should be performed within one Spring transaction.
The MAIN update is the first batch operation within that transaction.
ONE SPRING TRANSACTION

    ┌───────────────────────┐
    │ Batch UPDATE MAIN     │
    │ ~2,000 records        │
    └───────────┬───────────┘
                ↓
         continue transaction
batchUpdate() itself does not commit the transaction. Spring's @Transactional controls the commit/rollback boundary.
Error Scenarios
Scenario
Expected behaviour
CSV cannot be read
Migration fails; no MAIN update
Duplicate entity ID
Migration fails before update
Missing entity ID in MAIN
Migration fails
Mandatory migration value missing
Migration fails
Invalid migration value
Migration fails
Unexpected record count
Migration fails
One record update fails
Entire transaction rolls back
Update count ≠ expected
Migration fails/rolls back
DB connection/database failure
Entire transaction rolls back
For a successful migration, every expected record should be updated exactly once.
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] CSV is read successfully.
[ ] CSV validation occurs before MAIN data modification.
[ ] Duplicate/missing/invalid records are detected.
[ ] Applicable columns are populated from migration input.
[ ] Unchanged columns remain untouched.
[ ] Business/data version is updated to target version.
[ ] MAIN update uses JDBC batch processing.
[ ] ~2,000 records are supported in the configured batch.
[ ] Update counts are validated.
[ ] Failure results in rollback of the transaction.
Jira 3 — Implement Migration Audit and Outbox Event Logging
Summary
Implement Migration Audit and Outbox Event Logging
Description
Overview
Implement migration audit and outbox event creation for successfully migrated MAIN records.
These operations must participate in the same Spring transaction as the MAIN data update.
The outbox/event log is created during migration, but Kafka publishing is intentionally not part of this transaction. Events will be manually published after reconciliation and verification.
Transaction Design
The migration should use:
@Transactional
public void migrateMainData(...) {
    updateMainData();
    insertMigrationAudit();
    insertMigrationEvents();
}
Conceptually:
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│           ONE SPRING TRANSACTION            │
│                                             │
│  1. Batch UPDATE MAIN                       │
│                 ↓                           │
│  2. Batch INSERT MIGRATION AUDIT            │
│                 ↓                           │
│  3. Batch INSERT OUTBOX EVENTS              │
│                                             │
│       All succeed → COMMIT                  │
│       Any failure  → ROLLBACK               │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────┘
All three operations must use the same Spring-managed DataSource/transaction manager.
Implementation Methods
updateMainData();

insertMigrationAudit();

insertMigrationEvents();
Repositories:
MigrationRepository
MigrationAuditRepository
Migration Audit
Record-level audit should contain at least:
audit_id
migration_id
entity_name
entity_id
from_schema_version
to_schema_version
from_data_version
to_data_version
status
migration_definition
migrated_at
migrated_by
error_code
error_message
The audit should identify which migration definition was used, for example:
CUSTOMER_V1_TO_V2
Outbox Events
For every successfully migrated record, create the required V2 event/outbox record.
The event should represent the migrated V2 state according to the existing outbox/event contract.
Kafka publishing is not performed by the migration transaction.
Migration
   ↓
Outbox Event Created
   ↓
Reconciliation
   ↓
Manual Publish Trigger
   ↓
Kafka
Error Scenarios
Scenario
Expected behaviour
MAIN update fails
Roll back entire transaction
Audit insert fails
Roll back MAIN + audit
Outbox insert fails
Roll back MAIN + audit + outbox
Audit record count mismatch
Roll back transaction
Outbox record count mismatch
Roll back transaction
Kafka unavailable
Migration DB transaction remains unaffected; publish later
Manual publishing fails
Events remain available for retry using existing mechanism
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Audit entries are created for migrated records.
[ ] Outbox events are created for migrated records.
[ ] MAIN update, audit insert and outbox insert use one transaction.
[ ] All three operations use JDBC batch processing.
[ ] Failure of any operation rolls back the transaction.
[ ] Kafka is not invoked as part of the migration transaction.
[ ] Outbox events can be manually published after reconciliation.
Jira 4 — Apply Final Schema Constraints
Summary
Apply Final Constraints After Successful Schema Migration
Description
Overview
Apply the final target-schema constraints only after all existing production data has been successfully migrated and reconciled.
During preparation/data migration, newly introduced mandatory or conditional mandatory columns remain permissive to allow existing records to be populated.
Flow
MAIN Data Migration
        ↓
Reconciliation Successful
        ↓
Apply Final V2 Constraints
        ↓
Migration Complete
Implementation Methods
applySchemaConstraints(
        String entity,
        int targetSchemaVersion);
The method should apply target-version constraints based on the migration definition/schema requirements.
Examples:
NOT NULL
CHECK
required relationships
conditional mandatory rules implemented at DB level where applicable
Error Handling
Constraint application failure → migration remains incomplete.
Migration must not be marked COMPLETED.
Failure details must be recorded in migration control/audit.
Do not proceed to normal BAU until the schema is confirmed valid.
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Constraints are applied only after successful reconciliation.
[ ] All migrated records satisfy the final constraints.
[ ] Constraint failures are detected.
[ ] Migration is not marked complete if constraints cannot be applied.
