One architectural point I'd make explicit across the Jiras
Don't make the Jira sequence imply that Jira 2 commits MAIN data and Jira 3 later creates audit/outbox records.
They are separate implementation units, but at runtime they form one transaction:
                MIGRATE MAIN DATA
                       │
                       ▼
        ┌──────────────────────────────┐
        │      ONE TRANSACTION         │
        │                              │
        │  1. Update MAIN              │
        │  2. Insert Migration Audit   │
        │  3. Insert Outbox Events     │
        │                              │
        │  SUCCESS → COMMIT            │
        │  FAILURE → ROLLBACK          │
        └──────────────┬───────────────┘
                       │
                       ▼
                RECONCILIATION
                       │
                       ▼
              APPLY CONSTRAINTS
                       │
                       ▼
               MIGRATION COMPLETE
                       │
                       ▼
                  RESUME BAU
                       │
                       ▼
             MANUAL EVENT PUBLISH
This is important because otherwise someone reading the Jira might interpret Jira 2 = update and commit MAIN, followed by Jira 3 = audit/outbox, which would violate the atomicity we've designed.
I would also add one common technical note to Jira 2 and Jira 3
Transaction boundary: updateMainData(), insertMigrationAudit(), and insertMigrationEvents() are executed within a single Spring-managed transaction. All operations use the same transaction-managed datasource. Any runtime failure in these operations causes the complete transaction to roll back.
This gives your developers very clear implementation guidance without over-specifying the JDBC implementation beyond what we have already agreed.

