Jira Summary
Implement Transactional Bulk Data Migration, Audit and Event Publish Log
Description
Implement the application-driven data migration after the DB pre-migration preparation has been completed and verified by Prod Support.
The migration service will execute the three database operations as one Spring transaction:
Batch update MAIN data.
Batch insert migration audit records.
Batch insert event publish log records.
All three operations must succeed for the transaction to commit. Any failure must roll back the complete transaction.
The migration configuration is already loaded during application startup. The migration API trigger will invoke the migration service after the DB preparation steps have been verified.
Migration Flow
Suosys Job
    ↓
Migration API
    ↓
Validate Migration Input
    ↓
Migration Service
    ↓
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│          ONE SPRING TRANSACTION         │
│                                         │
│  1. updateMainData()                    │
│       ↓                                 │
│  2. insertMigrationAudit()              │
│       ↓                                 │
│  3. insertEventPublishLog()             │
│                                         │
│  All succeed → COMMIT                   │
│  Any failure   → ROLLBACK               │
└───────────────────┬─────────────────────┘
                    ↓
          Apply Final Constraints
                    ↓
          Generate Reconciliation
Scope
1. Validate migration input
Before executing the transactional data migration:
Validate requested source and target versions against the loaded migration configuration.
Validate migration CSV.
Validate mandatory migration values.
Validate duplicate records.
Validate record coverage against MAIN.
Ensure migration input is complete before any MAIN data is modified.
2. Batch update MAIN
Implement:
updateMainData();
Use JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate() to update the existing MAIN records.
The update should:
Populate new/replacement fields from migration input.
Leave unchanged fields untouched.
Set the target business/data version.
Update only records belonging to the expected source version.
Use parameterized SQL.
Validate update counts.
For the current migration, approximately 2,000 records will be processed in a batch.
3. Batch insert migration audit
Implement:
insertMigrationAudit();
Insert one migration audit record for each successfully migrated MAIN record.
Audit should capture information such as:
Migration ID
Entity
Entity ID
From schema version
To schema version
From data version
To data version
Migration status
Migration definition
Migration timestamp
Migrated by
Use:
jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate()
4. Batch insert event publish log
Implement:
insertEventPublishLog();
Insert one event publish-log record for each migrated record using the existing event publishing contract.
The event should represent the migrated V2 state that needs to be published downstream.
Use:
jdbcTemplate.batchUpdate()
Kafka publishing itself is not part of this transaction.
The event publish log will be used later by the existing/manual publishing process.
Transaction Requirement
The three operations must execute within one Spring transaction:
@Transactional
public void migrateMainData(
        List<MigrationDataRow> rows) {

    updateMainData(rows);

    insertMigrationAudit(rows);

    insertEventPublishLog(rows);
}
Conceptually:
ONE TRANSACTION
      │
      ├── Batch UPDATE MAIN
      │
      ├── Batch INSERT Migration Audit
      │
      └── Batch INSERT Event Publish Log
               │
               ↓
          COMMIT
If any operation fails:
MAIN UPDATE
    ↓
AUDIT INSERT
    ↓
EVENT PUBLISH LOG INSERT
    ↓
ERROR
    ↓
ROLLBACK ALL
Error Scenarios
The implementation must handle:
Invalid migration configuration → migration does not start.
Invalid CSV → migration does not start.
Duplicate CSV record → migration does not start.
Missing MAIN record → migration does not start.
Missing mandatory migration value → migration does not start.
MAIN update failure → complete transaction rolls back.
Unexpected MAIN update count → complete transaction rolls back.
Migration audit insert failure → MAIN changes roll back.
Audit count mismatch → transaction rolls back.
Event publish-log insert failure → MAIN and audit changes roll back.
Event publish-log count mismatch → transaction rolls back.
Database failure → complete transaction rolls back.
Kafka unavailable → no impact on this transaction, since Kafka publishing happens separately.
Suggested Methods
validateMigrationInput();

updateMainData();

insertMigrationAudit();

insertEventPublishLog();

validateUpdateCounts();

reconcileMigration();
The three atomic DB operations are therefore explicitly:
updateMainData()
insertMigrationAudit()
insertEventPublishLog()
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Migration input is validated before MAIN data is modified.
[ ] MAIN data is updated using JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate().
[ ] Migration audit records are inserted using JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate().
[ ] Event publish-log records are inserted using JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate().
[ ] All three operations execute within one Spring transaction.
[ ] Successful completion of all three operations results in COMMIT.
[ ] Failure of any operation results in ROLLBACK of all three operations.
[ ] MAIN update count is validated.
[ ] Audit record count is validated.
[ ] Event publish-log record count is validated.
[ ] Target business/data version is set during MAIN migration.
[ ] Unchanged MAIN fields are not modified.
[ ] Kafka publishing is not performed inside the migration transaction.
[ ] Event publish-log records remain available for subsequent/manual publishing.
[ ] Implementation is reusable for future schema/data migrations such as V2→V3.


Absolutely. I’d update the reconciliation Jira to make it explicitly different from the Day-0 load reconciliation, especially because only the migrated/updated fields are changed during this migration.
Jira Summary
Implement Migration Reconciliation Report and Migration Completion
Description
Implement reconciliation for the schema/data migration after the transactional data migration and final constraint application.
The reconciliation report should follow the existing Day-0 data load reconciliation report format, but with migration-specific checks and additional summary counts.
The reconciliation must compare only the fields being updated as part of the migration. Existing fields that are not part of the migration must not be compared because they are intentionally left untouched.
Reconciliation Flow
Transactional Data Migration
        ↓
Apply Final NOT NULL Constraints
        ↓
Generate Migration Reconciliation Report
        ↓
Product Team Verification
        ↓
Verification Successful
        ↓
Migration Complete
        ↓
Manual Event Publishing via Ausysys
Reconciliation Report
Generate a CSV report similar to the existing Day-0 data load reconciliation report.
For each migrated record, the report should contain two entries:
1. Source
   ↓
   V1 value

2. Target
   ↓
   V2 value
The report should include:
Entity/record identifier
Field/column name
Source value
Target value
Error column
Migration status as applicable
Example:
record_id | type   | field_b | field_c | error
----------|--------|---------|---------|------
1001      | SOURCE | old-B   | old-C   |
1001      | TARGET | new-B   | new-C   |
If a migrated field does not match the expected target value:
record_id | type   | field_b | field_c | error
----------|--------|---------|---------|----------------------
1001      | SOURCE | old-B   | old-C   |
1001      | TARGET | wrong-B | new-C   | field_b mismatch
Fields to Reconcile
The reconciliation must compare only the applicable/migrated fields defined in the migration configuration.
For example:
V1 MAIN
 ├── existing_field_1  ← NOT changed
 ├── existing_field_2  ← NOT changed
 ├── field_A           ← replaced
 └── data_version

Migration
 └── field_A → field_B + field_C

Reconciliation
 ├── field_B ✓
 ├── field_C ✓
 └── existing_field_1 / existing_field_2 → NOT compared
This prevents false reconciliation failures for fields that were intentionally not modified.
The applicable fields should be driven by the loaded migration configuration rather than hard-coded separately in the reconciliation logic.
Summary Section
The reconciliation summary must include the following counts:
Migration Reconciliation Summary

--------------------------------------
Expected Data Updates     : 2,000
Successful Data Updates   : 2,000
Failed Data Updates       : 0

Migration Audit Count     : 2,000
Event Publish Log Count   : 2,000

Reconciliation Success   : YES
--------------------------------------
This differs from the existing Day-0 load reconciliation, where the summary primarily reports the MAIN table load count.
For schema/data migration, the summary must reconcile three related outputs:
Data Update Count
       +
Migration Audit Count
       +
Event Publish Log Count
Expected relationship:
Data Updates = Migration Audit Records = Event Publish Log Records
Reconciliation Checks
The reconciliation should verify:
Expected migration records = CSV records.
Successful MAIN data updates = expected records.
Failed MAIN updates = 0.
Migration audit count = successful MAIN update count.
Event publish-log count = successful MAIN update count.
Each expected record exists in MAIN.
Target data version is correct.
Only configured applicable fields are compared.
Each applicable field contains the expected target value.
Mandatory migrated fields contain valid values.
Any mismatch is captured in the report error column.
Existing/non-migrated fields are not included in value comparison.
Method Structure
Suggested methods:
reconcileMigration();

generateReconciliationReport();

reconcileUpdatedFields();

validateMigrationCounts();

validateAuditCount();

validateEventPublishLogCount();

completeMigration();
The main flow would be:
public void reconcileMigration(
        String entity,
        List<MigrationDataRow> rows) {

    validateMigrationCounts();

    validateAuditCount();

    validateEventPublishLogCount();

    reconcileUpdatedFields();

    generateReconciliationReport();
}
Error Handling
If reconciliation finds any mismatch:
Reconciliation Failure
        ↓
Generate report with errors
        ↓
Do NOT mark migration COMPLETE
        ↓
Product Team investigates
        ↓
Corrective action / rerun as applicable
Examples of reconciliation errors:
MAIN update count mismatch.
Audit count mismatch.
Event publish-log count mismatch.
Expected target value not present in MAIN.
Mandatory migrated field missing.
Incorrect target data version.
Unexpected record updated.
Updated field value mismatch.
Acceptance Criteria
[ ] Reconciliation CSV follows the existing Day-0 data load reconciliation report format.
[ ] Each migrated record produces source and target entries.
[ ] Report contains an error column for MAIN data-update/reconciliation errors.
[ ] Reconciliation compares only fields updated by the migration.
[ ] Existing fields that are not modified by migration are excluded from value comparison.
[ ] Applicable fields are driven by migration configuration.
[ ] Summary contains expected/successful/failed data update counts.
[ ] Summary contains migration audit count.
[ ] Summary contains event publish-log count.
[ ] Data update count, audit count and event publish-log count are reconciled.
[ ] Target data version is verified.
[ ] Field-level mismatches are captured in the CSV.
[ ] Reconciliation report is generated before Product Team verification.
[ ] Migration is not marked complete when reconciliation fails.
[ ] Successful reconciliation allows migration completion and subsequent manual event publishing.

